nodecode-hyprland is a rewrite of mddragon18's mddragon18/hyprland-computer-use:

  https://github.com/mddragon18/hyprland-computer-use
  commit eef766e18200338b772b14c20dbf03d603c84982
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 mddragon18 (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's `hyprland` folder -- its Hermes computer-use backend,
which answered capture, click, drag, scroll, type, key, focus_app, list_apps
and list_windows. Hermes's own computer_use tool is gone here, so the same
nine actions are one contract on one `hyprland` tool, and the same nine are
Lisp verbs in the HYPRLAND package for eval. The coordinate contract is the
original's, field for field in spirit: a capture records its origin and its
scale, every coordinate the model sends is read in that frame's pixels and
mapped back to native ones, a capture of a named window focuses it, crops to
it and makes it the target, and input outside the target is refused with the
window's native box instead of landing in the tile next door. The ruler over
a capture, the downscale to a capture width, the wl-copy paste fallback for
text a command line would read as an option, the super-is-logo key alias and
the wtype -M/-k/-m argv are the Python's own, in Common Lisp. Every CLI call
is the fixed argv the Python built: grim -g, hyprctl clients/monitors/repl,
wtype, wl-copy, magick and the pointer helper's hitat/drag/scrollat/scroll.

Left out: the plugin's Python packaging, its plugin.yaml manifest, its
pytest and smoke suites and install.sh, which have no meaning outside Hermes;
and the C source of the pointer helper itself, which is not vendored here --
the cell runs the hypr-vinput binary built from the original repository's
pointer/ folder (the catalog rule that keeps binaries out of the repository),
named in pointer_bin or found on PATH. Added, because a Nodecode cell is
called more than one way: one tool with an action field, the nine verbs, a
/hyprland command that prints the doctor or the windows, (help :hyprland),
and a section whose members are the settings the Python read from Hermes's
config (capture_width, grid, ruler_step, scope, enforce_target) plus
pointer_bin and capture_dir.
